    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Adenocarcinoma of the gallbladder invading the liver (C23)</content>. CT and MRI show a 7.1 cm mass arising from the gallbladder fundus and invading liver segments IVb and V, with gallstones and two enlarged hepatoduodenal ligament nodes (1.6 and 1.3 cm); the bile ducts are not dilated, the main portal vein and hepatic artery are free, and there are no other liver lesions. The liver is smooth, without cirrhosis. CT of the chest shows only a 6 mm calcified granuloma in the right upper lobe. The core biopsy shows moderately differentiated adenocarcinoma, CK7 positive, CK20 and CDX2 negative, consistent with a pancreatobiliary origin; with the imaging, this is a primary gallbladder adenocarcinoma, not a primary liver cancer. Alpha-fetoprotein is normal and CA 19-9 412 U/mL. Clinical T3 N1 M0 (stage IIIB) on imaging. Liver function is preserved: Child-Pugh A6 (albumin 3.4 g/dL), ALBI grade 2, bilirubin and INR normal. Dr. Choi recommends tumor board review and a staging laparoscopy before any attempt at resection, because peritoneal disease is often not seen on CT in this cancer, and genomic profiling of the biopsy tissue. He agreed. [S]<list><item>Tumor board review in two days.</item><item>Staging laparoscopy, with an implanted port under the same anesthetic, in about ten days.</item><item>Comprehensive genomic profiling of the biopsy tissue.</item><item>Specialist palliative care introduced; he would like to meet the team once staging is complete.</item><item>Acetaminophen and small frequent meals; dietitian appointment.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Right upper quadrant abdominal mass with weight loss (R19.01)</content>. Superseded by the biopsy diagnosis of gallbladder adenocarcinoma. [S]<list><item>Problem resolved.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Resolved hepatitis B infection (Z86.19)</content>. Hepatitis B surface antigen negative, total core antibody positive, surface antibody 85 mIU/mL, and HBV DNA undetectable (drawn with the CT): resolved past infection with no liver damage. Hepatitis C antibody negative. Chemotherapy and immunotherapy carry a small risk of reactivation; monitoring with prompt treatment is reasonable rather than routine antiviral prophylaxis. [S]<list><item>HBV DNA and ALT every 3 months during treatment and for 6 to 12 months after.</item><item>Entecavir at once if HBV DNA becomes detectable or surface antigen reappears.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Multiphase contrast CT or MRI of the liver</td><td>At presentation of a liver mass</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Mass arises from the gallbladder and invades segments IVb and V; non-cirrhotic liver; no other liver lesions</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>CT of the chest, abdomen and pelvis</td><td>At staging, then every 2 to 3 months on treatment</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Two enlarged hepatoduodenal ligament nodes; no distant disease on CT; 6 mm calcified lung granuloma</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Core biopsy of the mass</td><td>When imaging cannot make the diagnosis (non-cirrhotic liver)</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Adenocarcinoma, CK7 positive, CK20 and CDX2 negative: pancreatobiliary origin</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Alpha-fetoprotein</td><td>At presentation of a liver mass</td><td>Up to date</td><td>3.2 ng/mL at intake; normal</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>CA 19-9 (and CEA when raised)</td><td>At diagnosis, then with each cycle or restaging</td><td>Up to date</td><td>CA 19-9 412 U/mL and CEA 8.6 ng/mL at intake</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Liver function: Child-Pugh and ALBI</td><td>At diagnosis, then before each treatment decision</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Child-Pugh A6; ALBI grade 2; bilirubin 0.9 mg/dL and INR 1.0 at intake</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Hepatitis B and C serology</td><td>Before chemotherapy or immunotherapy</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Surface antigen negative, core antibody positive, surface antibody positive, HBV DNA undetectable; hepatitis C antibody negative</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>ECOG performance status</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>ECOG 1</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Weight and nutrition</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>60.6 kg, down 0.4 kg in two weeks; dietitian booked</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Pain</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>2 of 10 on acetaminophen</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Multidisciplinary tumor board</td><td>Before treatment of a biliary tract cancer</td><td>Ordered</td><td>In two days</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Staging laparoscopy</td><td>Before resection of gallbladder cancer</td><td>Ordered</td><td>In about ten days, with a port</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Comprehensive genomic profiling of tumor tissue</td><td>At diagnosis of advanced disease</td><td>Ordered</td><td>Sent on the biopsy tissue</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Medical oncology</td><td>At diagnosis</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Seen by Dr. Choi today</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Specialist palliative care</td><td>From the diagnosis of advanced cancer</td><td>Due</td><td>Introduced today; first meeting after staging, at his choice</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Tissue diagnosis and complete staging within four weeks of the first visit. In progress; target 1924-06-01 [S]</item><item>Right upper abdominal pain at 3 of 10 or less, with full days of work possible (ECOG 0 to 1). In progress; target 1924-10-26 [S]</item><item>Weight held within 2 kg of the weight at the start of treatment. In progress; target 1924-10-26 [S]</item><item>Goals of care and a health care agent documented, and revisited as treatment changes. In progress; target 1924-09-21 [S]</item><item>Complete first-line chemoimmunotherapy without grade 3 or higher toxicity. In progress; target 1924-10-26 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: NCCN Clinical Practice Guidelines in Oncology: Biliary Tract Cancers (2026); AASLD Practice Guidance on prevention, diagnosis, and treatment of hepatocellular carcinoma (2023); Hepatitis B Virus Screening and Management for Patients With Cancer Prior to Therapy: ASCO Provisional Clinical Opinion Update (2020); Integration of Palliative Care Into Standard Oncology Care: American Society of Clinical Oncology Clinical Practice Guideline Update (2017)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Tumor board in two days; staging laparoscopy in about ten days. Visit in two weeks with the result and the tumor board recommendation. Next scheduled visit 1924-06-01: staging laparoscopy result and tumor board recommendation; first cycle of chemoimmunotherapy and specialist palliative care. [S]</paragraph></text>

